@node Lexical conventions
@nodedescription Lexical conventions used in Scheme programs.
@chapter Lexical conventions

This section gives an informal account of some of the lexical
conventions used in writing Scheme programs.  For a formal syntax of
Scheme, @pxref{Formal syntax}.

@node Identifiers
@section Identifiers
An identifier is any sequence of letters, digits, and @quotedbl{extended
identifier characters} provided that it does not have a prefix which is
a valid number.  However, the @samp{.}  token (a single period) used in
the list syntax is not an identifier.

All implementations of Scheme must support the following extended
identifier characters:

@indentedblock
@multitable @columnfractions 1
@item @t{! $ % @ampchar{} * + - . / : < = > ? @atchar{} ^ _ ~}
@end multitable
@end indentedblock

Alternatively, an identifier can be represented by a sequence of zero or
more characters enclosed within vertical lines (@samp{|}), analogous to
string literals.  Any character, including whitespace characters, but
excluding the backslash and vertical line characters, can appear
verbatim in such an identifier.  In addition, characters can be
specified using either an @angle{inline hex escape} or the same escapes
available in strings.

For example, the identifier @hc{|H\x65;llo|} is the same identifier as
@nc{Hello}, and in an implementation that supports the appropriate
Unicode character the identifier @hc{|\x3BB;|} is the same as the
identifier @code{@U{03BB}}.  What is more, @hc{|\t\t|} and
@hc{|\x9;\x9;|} are the same.  Note that @hc{||} is a valid identifier
that is different from any other identifier.

Here are some examples of identifiers:

@indentedblock
@multitable @columnfractions .62 .38
@item @t{...} @tab @t{+}
@item @t{+soup+} @tab @t{<=?}
@item @t{.>string} @tab @t{a34kTMNs}
@item @t{lambda} @tab @t{list->vector}
@item @t{q} @tab @t{V17a}
@item @t{|two words|} @tab @t{|two@identesc{@backslashchar{}x20;}words|}
@item @nt{the-word-recursion-has-many-meanings}
@end multitable
@end indentedblock

@xref{Lexical structure} for the formal syntax of identifiers.

Identifiers have two uses within Scheme programs:

@itemize @bullet
@item
Any identifier can be used as a variable or as a syntactic keyword
(@pxref{Variables syntactic keywords and regions}; @pxref{Macros}).

@item
When an identifier appears as a literal or within a literal
(@pxref{Literal expressions}), it is being used to denote a
@emph{symbol} (@pxref{Symbols}).
@end itemize

@cindex case sensitivity
In contrast with earlier revisions of the
report@footnote{@value{refR5RS}}, the syntax distinguishes between upper
and lower case in identifiers and in characters specified using their
names.  However, it does not distinguish between upper and lower case in
numbers, nor in @angle{inline hex escapes} used in the syntax of
identifiers, characters, or strings.  None of the identifiers defined in
this report contain upper-case characters, even when they appear to do
so as a result of the English-language convention of capitalizing the
first word of a sentence.

@cindex directives
@cindex atmosphere @subentry directives @seeentry{directives}
The following directives give explicit control over case folding.

@indentedblock
@multitable @columnfractions 1
@item @t{@hashchar{}!fold-case}
@item @t{@hashchar{}!no-fold-case}
@end multitable
@end indentedblock

These directives can appear anywhere comments are permitted
(@pxref{Whitespace and comments}) but must be followed by a delimiter.
They are treated as comments, except that they affect the reading of
subsequent data from the same port.  The @nc{@hashchar{}!fold-case}
directive causes subsequent identifiers and character names to be
case-folded as if by @nc{string-foldcase} (@pxref{Strings}).  It has no
effect on character literals.  The @nc{@hashchar{}!no-fold-case}
directive causes a return to the default, non-folding behavior.

@node Whitespace and comments
@cindex comments
@cindex whitespace
@cindex atmosphere @subentry comments @seeentry{comments}
@cindex atmosphere @subentry whitespace @seeentry{whitespace}
@section Whitespace and comments
@define{Whitespace} characters include the space, tab, and newline
characters.  (Implementations may provide additional whitespace
characters such as page break.)  Whitespace is used for improved
readability and as necessary to separate tokens from each other, a token
being an indivisible lexical unit such as an identifier or number, but
is otherwise insignificant.  Whitespace can occur between any two
tokens, but not within a token.  Whitespace occurring inside a string or
inside a symbol delimited by vertical lines is significant.

The lexical syntax includes several comment forms.  Comments are treated
exactly like whitespace.

A semicolon (@samp{;}) indicates the start of a line comment.  The
comment continues to the end of the line on which the semicolon appears.
Another way to indicate a comment is to prefix a @angle{datum}
(@pxref{External representations}) with @samp{@hashchar{};} and optional
@angle{whitespace}.  The comment consists of the comment prefix
@samp{@hashchar{};}, the space, and the @angle{datum} together.  This
notation is useful for @quotedbl{commenting out} sections of code.

Block comments are indicated with properly nested
@samp{@hashchar{}@U{007C}} and @samp{@U{007C}@hashchar{}} pairs.

@lisp
#|
   The FACT procedure computes the factorial
   of a non-negative integer.
|#
(define fact
  (lambda (n)
    (if (= n 0)
        #;(= n 1)
        1        ;Base case: return 1
        (* n (fact (- n 1))))))
@end lisp

@node Other notations
@section Other notations
For a description of the notations used for numbers, @pxref{Numbers}.

@table @asis{}
@item @samp{.}@t{@ @ }@samp{+}@t{@ @ }@samp{-}
These are used in numbers, and can also occur anywhere in an identifier.
A delimited plus or minus sign by itself is also an identifier.  A
delimited period (not occurring within a number or identifier) is used
in the notation for pairs (@pxref{Pairs and lists}), and to indicate a
rest-parameter in a formal parameter list (@pxref{Procedures}).  Note
that a sequence of two or more periods @emph{is} an identifier.

@item @samp{@U{0028}}@t{@ @ }@samp{@U{0029}}
Parentheses are used for grouping and to notate lists (@pxref{Pairs and
lists}).

@item @samp{'}
The apostrophe (single quote) character is used to indicate literal data
(@pxref{Literal expressions}).

@item @samp{`}
The grave accent (backquote) character is used to indicate partly
constant data (@pxref{Quasiquotation}).

@item @samp{@comma{}}@t{@ @ }@samp{@comma{}@atchar{}}
The character comma and the sequence comma at-sign are used in
conjunction with quasiquotation (@pxref{Quasiquotation}).

@item @samp{"}
The quotation mark character is used to delimit strings
(@pxref{Strings}).

@item @samp{@backslashchar{}}
Backslash is used in the syntax for character constants
(@pxref{Characters}) and as an escape character within string constants
(@pxref{Strings}) and identifiers (@pxref{Lexical structure}).

@item @samp{@U{005B}}@t{@ @ }@samp{@U{005D}}@t{@ @ }@samp{@lbracechar{}}@t{@ @ }@samp{@rbracechar{}}
Left and right square and curly brackets (braces) are reserved for
possible future extensions to the language.

@item @samp{@hashchar{}}
The number sign is used for a variety of purposes depending on the
character that immediately follows it:

@item @samp{@hashchar{}t}@t{@ @ }@samp{@hashchar{}f}
These are the boolean constants (@pxref{Booleans}), along with the
alternatives @samp{#true} @samp{#false}.

@item @samp{@hashchar{}@backslashchar{}}
This introduces a character constant (@pxref{Characters}).

@item @samp{@hashchar{}@U{0028}}
This introduces a vector constant (@pxref{Vectors}).  Vector constants
are terminated by @samp{@U{0029}}.

@item @samp{@hashchar{}u8@U{0028}}
This introduces a bytevector constant (@pxref{Vectors}).  Bytevector
constants are terminated by @samp{@U{0029}}.

@item @samp{@hashchar{}e}@t{@ @ }@samp{@hashchar{}i}@t{@ @ }@samp{@hashchar{}b}@t{@ @ }@samp{@hashchar{}o}@t{@ @ }@samp{@hashchar{}d}@t{@ @ }@samp{@hashchar{}x}
These are used in the notation for numbers (@pxref{Syntax of numerical
constants}).

@item @samp{@hashchar{}@sv{n}=}@t{@ @ }@samp{@hashchar{}@sv{n}@hashchar{}}
These are used for labeling and referencing other literal data
(@pxref{Datum labels}).
@end table

@node Datum labels
@cindex datum label
@section Datum labels
@defblock
@deftypeline {lexical syntax} @/ @
@datumlabel{@hashchar{}}@sv{n}@datumlabel{=}@sv{datum}
@deftypeline {lexical syntax} @/ @
@datumref{@hashchar{}}@sv{n}@datumref{@hashchar{}}

The lexical syntax @samp{@prosedatumlabel{n}@angle{datum}} reads the
same as @samp{@angle{datum}}, but also results in @samp{@angle{datum}}
being labeled by @samp{@angle{n}}.  It is an error if @samp{@angle{n}}
is not a sequence of digits.

@cindex datum reference
@cindex circular structure
@cindex shared structure
The lexical syntax @samp{@prosedatumref{n}} serves as a reference to
some object labeled by @samp{@prosedatumlabel{n}}; the result is the
same object as the @samp{@prosedatumlabel{n}} (@pxref{Equivalence
predicates}).  Together, these syntaxes permit the notation of
structures with shared or circular substructure.

@lisp
(let ((x (list 'a 'b 'c)))
  (set-cdr! (cddr x) x)
  x)                       @ok{#0=(a b c . #0#)}
@end lisp

The scope of a datum label is the portion of the outermost datum in
which it appears that is to the right of the label.  Consequently, a
reference @samp{@prosedatumref{n}} can occur only after a label
@samp{@prosedatumlabel{n}}; it is an error to attempt a forward
reference.  In addition, it is an error if the reference appears as the
labeled object itself (as in @ns{@prosedatumlabel{n}
@prosedatumref{n}}), because the object labeled by
@samp{@prosedatumlabel{n}} is not well defined in this case.

@cindex datum reference @subentry circular datum reference
It is an error for a @angle{program} or @angle{library} to include
circular references except in literals.  In particular, it is an error
for @nc{quasiquote} (@pxref{Quasiquotation}) to contain them.

@lisp
#1=(begin (display #\x) #1#) @lispnote{error}
@end lisp
@end defblock
